# Summary
The commit switched response building to hyper and now panics on invalid header values. Because response override query parameters are not validated, a crafted request can induce a panic, resulting in a denial-of-service for the connection (and potentially the process depending on panic settings).
The new hyper-based response conversion unconditionally applies response header overrides from query parameters and then calls `http::Response::builder().header(...)` for each header. Query parameters are percent-decoded without filtering control characters, so an attacker who can issue a GET with `response-*` parameters can inject invalid header bytes (e.g., CR/LF). Hyper treats these as invalid and stores an error in the builder; the subsequent `.body(...).expect(...)` panics. This panic aborts the request task (and could terminate the process if built with panic=abort), making a remotely triggerable availability issue. Previously, the tiny_http response builder ignored invalid headers instead of panicking, so the crash is newly introduced.

